# The routing panel behind the LOCALLY RUN Request Handler, on prs / tim.
# Captured 2026-09-10, handler v1.0.1, block server from commit 14c065a.
# Companion to routing-panel-deployed.capture.log: same instrument, same project, same
# nodes, so the two files diff directly. That diff is the point of this file.

$ contensis connect prs && contensis set project tim && contensis list blocks
  website [main]: running        (version 6 at the time of capture)

$ node apps/website/server/index.ts                      # block server on :3001
$ contensis dev requests website http://localhost:3001 --args --port=5001

# --- WHAT THE HANDLER SETS LOCALLY ---
#
# Of the fifteen headers observed on the deployed block, exactly ONE arrives locally.
# These are hard absences, not values the panel withheld: every header below is on the
# panel's own lists, so an absent one renders as null rather than falling into
# otherHeaderNames.

  header                       deployed                     local
  ---------------------------- ---------------------------- ----------------------------
  x-node-id                    set                          SET
  x-entry-id                   set                          SET
  traceparent                  W3C trace id                 SET
  x-orig-host                  staging-tim-prs.cloud...     absent
  x-node-versionstatus         published                    absent
  x-entry-versionstatus        published                    absent
  x-alias                      prs                          absent
  delivery-project             "" (empty string)            absent (null, not "")
  x-requires-alias             true                         absent
  x-requires-project-api-id    true                         absent
  x-requires-node-id           true                         absent
  x-requires-entry-id          true                         absent
  x-requires-block-id          true                         absent
  x-forwarded-proto            https                        absent
  use-modern-cache-logic       true                         absent
  surrogate-key                true                         absent
  x-block-request              pass                         absent

  otherHeaderNames, local:    ["content-length"]
  otherHeaderNames, deployed: ["content-length", "cookie", "x-forwarded-for",
                               "x-geoip-country-code"]

# The emptiness IS the finding. The handler adds only x-node-id, x-entry-id, traceparent
# and content-length. Everything else on the deployed side is CDN and IIS layer, and none
# of it exists locally.
#
# host differs too, and is worth noting for anyone building absolute URLs:
#   local:    localhost
#   deployed: v5-main-website-tim-prs-block-hq2-blue.blocks.contensis.com
# so neither is the public hostname. Deployed, x-orig-host carries it. Locally, nothing
# does.

# --- THE PATH IS DROPPED, UNCONDITIONALLY ---

$ curl http://localhost:5001/blogs/canvas
  panel request.pathname : /            <- not /blogs/canvas
  panel request.url      : /
  resolution.identity    : kind=id value=c877264a-787f-4b0d-8045-1ec8ee2d3d4e source=header
  resolution.entryId     : ebec630b-67ef-4ccb-ae5d-11679f4719eb

$ curl http://localhost:5001/
  resolution.identity    : kind=id value=97ad4a16-821a-4798-adcd-f17ce876538a source=header
  resolution.entryId     : c615ce7b-de7b-440b-91c6-a471e5cb50a8

# Both node ids match the CMS and match what the DEPLOYED block resolved for the same two
# paths. So identity routing is faithful locally; the path is not. Deployed, the same
# request arrives as pathname /blogs/canvas.
#
# NEW: the block's manifest.json has enableFullUriRouting: true, and the path is STILL
# dropped locally. So the local divergence is unconditional, not a consequence of the flag
# being read as false. The contract's "enableFullUriRouting is hardcoded false in the
# override path" explains the mechanism, but the observable outcome does not depend on the
# manifest at all.

# --- CONTROL: no handler, straight to the block server ---

$ curl http://localhost:3001/blogs/canvas
  panel request.pathname : /blogs/canvas
  resolution.identity    : kind=path value=/blogs/canvas source=path
  otherHeaderNames       : []

# The path survives and identity falls back to the path, which is what distinguishes
# "the handler dropped it" from "the app lost it". host/user-agent/accept are curl's in
# both cases, so the handler is not the source of those.

# --- x-requires-* ARE CLIENT-SET, AND MY EARLIER ATTRIBUTION WAS WRONG ---
#
# routing-panel-deployed.capture.log finding 3 says five x-requires-* arrive on the
# deployed block "without any client sending them", and the commit message for that file
# attributed them to "the handler itself". The second half is wrong.
#
# Tested here by sending three of them explicitly through the local handler:
#   - the block received exactly those three (value "true"); the other four stayed null
#   - the handler emitted the matching RESPONSE headers: x-alias: prs,
#     x-project-api-id: tim, x-node-id: c877264a-..., plus an unrequested x-version-no: 6
#
# So the documented behaviour is correct: they are client-set echo-request headers the
# handler forwards. On the deployed block something upstream, most likely the CDN layer,
# is what sends them. Not the handler, and not the app.
# (This test was run in the same session; its raw output was not kept, unlike the panel
# JSON below. The three-of-seven split and the response headers are as observed.)
#
# Also note packages/routing/src/fixtures/inboundHeaders.capture.log shows five
# x-requires-* behind a local handler. That was a curl sending them by hand. It is not
# evidence of the handler setting them, and it should not be read as such.

# --- WHAT THIS RUN CANNOT SETTLE ---
#
# x-alias came back null locally, which is equally consistent with "denylisted" and with
# "nothing sets it locally". So this file does NOT adjudicate the x-alias denylist dispute.
# That rests on the deployed observation (x-alias: prs arrived) plus the source read of
# DisallowedRequestHeaderMappings, both recorded in the deployed capture.

# --- HANDLER CONSOLE ---
#
# The documented GitHub 404 still fires and is still non-fatal. The binary was already
# pinned to v1.0.1 in ~/.contensis/cli-manifest.json on this machine, so no download was
# attempted. Port 5000 was never tried: --port=5001 was passed prophylactically, so the
# AirPlay collision is UNRETESTED here rather than reconfirmed.

[WARN] [fetch] non-retriable 404: Not Found https://api.github.com/repos/contensis/request-handler-localdevelopment/releases
Error: Unable to get releases
    at GitHubCliModuleProvider.FindLatestRelease (...)
    at async RequestHandlerFactory.CheckUpdate (...)
> Launching request handler for local development
info: Microsoft.Hosting.Lifetime[14]
      Now listening on: http://[::]:5001

# Beyond that the log is only HttpClient proxy traces. The handler logs NOTHING about node
# or renderer resolution, so there is no local trace of how /blogs/canvas became a node id.
# The header the block receives is the only evidence that step ran, which is a good reason
# to have an instrument in the block.

info: Microsoft.AspNetCore.Hosting.Diagnostics[2]
      Request finished HTTP/1.1 GET http://localhost:5001/blogs/canvas - 200 17630 text/html;+charset=utf-8 563.9974ms

